# Linux Navigation and File Management Study Notes

A clean, foundational guide covering command-line interface navigation, file systems, output redirection, and shell environments.

---

## 1. Linux File System Structure

Linux organizes data in a **hierarchical file system** structured like a tree. 

* **The Root Directory (`/`)**: Represented by a single forward slash. This is the main trunk of the system from which all other directories branch out.
* **The Home Directory (`~`)**: Represented by a tilde. This is your personal workspace where you have full permissions to create, modify, or delete files.
* **Current Working Directory**: The specific folder you are currently working inside. Any command executed without an explicit path applies directly to this location.
* **Terminology**: In Linux, a "directory" is the exact equivalent of a "folder" in a graphical operating system.

---

## 2. Navigation Commands

| Command | Action |
| :--- | :--- |
| `pwd` | **Print Working Directory**: Outputs the exact absolute path of your current location. |
| `echo ~` | Displays the explicit, full path string to your home directory. |
| `ls` | Basic listing; shows non-hidden files and folders inside your current location. |
| `ls ~` | Lists the contents of your home directory, regardless of where you currently are. |
| `cd project` | Moves into a subfolder named `project` (Uses a *relative path*). |
| `cd ..` | Moves up exactly one level to the parent directory. |
| `cd ~` | Navigates directly back to your home directory. |
| `cd /home/labex/project` | Navigates using an **Absolute Path** (An explicit location starting from the root `/`). |

